1
00:00:01,400 --> 00:00:04,763
You've probably done this. You type a message…

2
00:00:05,013 --> 00:00:06,324
…and something answers.

3
00:00:09,563 --> 00:00:12,356
You ask for help with a chore.

4
00:00:13,613 --> 00:00:21,250
Seconds later, there it is: a polite letter to your noisy neighbour. It really feels like talking to someone.

5
00:00:21,500 --> 00:00:25,033
But who, or what, is on the other side?

6
00:00:25,933 --> 00:00:29,667
Let's step back. Here you are, at your computer.

7
00:00:29,917 --> 00:00:33,450
Your message leaves through a cable in the wall…

8
00:00:33,700 --> 00:00:35,752
…and travels a long way…

9
00:00:36,002 --> 00:00:41,217
…to a data centre. It's tempting to picture a friendly face in there.

10
00:00:41,467 --> 00:00:43,148
Something powerful, always on.

11
00:00:44,367 --> 00:00:47,159
It reads your message and writes back.

12
00:00:47,409 --> 00:00:51,683
And it does this for millions of people, all at once.

13
00:00:54,400 --> 00:00:56,822
So let's take a closer look.

14
00:00:57,800 --> 00:00:59,111
A few screws…

15
00:01:01,200 --> 00:01:10,178
Behind the friendly face: LEDs and wires. The face was never real. What's real is inside: a machine. Let's go in.

16
00:01:13,433 --> 00:01:15,315
Part one. The machine.

17
00:01:18,350 --> 00:01:24,135
Take a sentence: it was raining outside, so he took a… what comes next?

18
00:01:24,385 --> 00:01:30,711
First, the text is cut into small pieces called tokens: whole words, or parts of words.

19
00:01:30,961 --> 00:01:35,235
The machine only works with numbers, so it uses a dictionary…

20
00:01:35,485 --> 00:01:43,292
…a fixed list of about fifty thousand tokens, each with its own number. Every token gets swapped for its number.

21
00:01:45,505 --> 00:01:46,446
That's tokenization.

22
00:01:48,100 --> 00:01:49,411
Next comes embedding.

23
00:01:49,661 --> 00:02:03,224
Each number goes through a big table, and comes out as a set of directions. Picture a stack of dials, each pointing its own way: seven hundred and sixty-eight of them, in a small model.

24
00:02:03,474 --> 00:02:07,748
That's where each token starts, before it has seen any context.

25
00:02:07,998 --> 00:02:10,420
Every token gets its own stack.

26
00:02:13,383 --> 00:02:19,909
Now, the heart of the machine: the transformer layers. Think of them as columns of cogs.

27
00:02:20,159 --> 00:02:22,211
Let's turn it by hand.

28
00:02:22,461 --> 00:02:27,476
With each turn, the dials enter a layer, and its cogs nudge them.

29
00:02:27,726 --> 00:02:30,148
The next layer nudges them again.

30
00:02:30,398 --> 00:02:31,339
And again.

31
00:02:31,998 --> 00:02:35,161
A real model does this dozens of times.

32
00:02:35,411 --> 00:02:39,885
No magic here: just a long series of small, mechanical steps.

33
00:02:40,785 --> 00:02:42,467
Then comes the output.

34
00:02:42,717 --> 00:02:47,191
Only the last row matters: it holds what should come next.

35
00:02:47,441 --> 00:02:53,196
It is compared with every token in the dictionary, and each token gets a probability.

36
00:02:53,446 --> 00:02:57,920
Read backwards, the dictionary turns numbers into words: hat, coat, bus…

37
00:02:58,170 --> 00:03:08,800
Now, a surprise. The machine doesn't always take the top choice. It draws one at random, weighted by those probabilities. That's the G in GPT: generative.

38
00:03:09,050 --> 00:03:12,583
It's why the same question can get different answers.

39
00:03:14,950 --> 00:03:16,461
This time: coat.

40
00:03:17,250 --> 00:03:22,835
The new token joins the sentence. One turn of the machine, one token out.

41
00:03:23,735 --> 00:03:30,061
Of course, nobody turns a crank. Electricity does the work, and takes a lot of it.

42
00:03:30,311 --> 00:03:32,733
And the machine simply starts again.

43
00:03:35,533 --> 00:03:46,504
One full pass for every token. It never writes a whole sentence, only the next token, over and over, until it produces a special token that means: stop.

44
00:03:46,754 --> 00:03:49,546
Then it waits for the next request.

45
00:03:49,796 --> 00:03:52,959
The text is packed up, and sent back.

46
00:03:53,759 --> 00:03:55,441
We leave the machine…

47
00:03:55,959 --> 00:03:58,011
…and follow the answer home.

48
00:03:58,759 --> 00:04:01,182
Through the network, along the cable…

49
00:04:02,359 --> 00:04:04,041
…and onto your screen.

50
00:04:04,291 --> 00:04:11,357
A conversation on one side. On the other, a very large machine, guessing one token at a time.

51
00:04:12,857 --> 00:04:19,013
But how does it use context? Take this sentence: he sat on the river bank.

52
00:04:19,263 --> 00:04:22,796
Inside each layer is a key mechanism called attention.

53
00:04:23,046 --> 00:04:35,498
Each token looks back at the tokens before it, never ahead. The thicker the link, the more that token matters. For "bank", it's "river" that counts most, and it nudges the dial.

54
00:04:36,946 --> 00:04:40,480
In reality, this happens for all tokens at once.

55
00:04:40,730 --> 00:04:42,411
And in every layer.

56
00:04:43,730 --> 00:04:46,352
What comes next? "To", then "fish".

57
00:04:46,930 --> 00:04:54,937
Now a second sentence: he put money in the bank. "Bank" is the same token, with exactly the same dials.

58
00:04:55,187 --> 00:04:58,350
But this time, it looks back at "money".

59
00:05:03,447 --> 00:05:06,069
And the prediction changes: "each month".

60
00:05:06,647 --> 00:05:12,232
Same token, same start, two different directions. That's how context flows through the machine.

61
00:05:13,932 --> 00:05:20,258
Context can reach very far back. Here's a small whodunit, with clues scattered through the text.

62
00:05:20,508 --> 00:05:22,190
Who is the murderer?

63
00:05:23,342 --> 00:05:27,445
Probably Colonel Mustard. Yet nobody taught it how to reason.

64
00:05:28,345 --> 00:05:33,360
All the text the machine can see at once is its context window.

65
00:05:33,610 --> 00:05:37,884
It writes the answer as always, one token at a time.

66
00:05:38,134 --> 00:05:48,534
To guess the name, it looks back across the whole window, and a few links weigh far more than others: the rain, the shoes, the word "dry".

67
00:05:48,784 --> 00:05:55,310
"Colonel" comes out on top. This is the essence of the machine: a good next-token guesser.

68
00:05:59,560 --> 00:06:06,627
And guessing well takes context, clues and facts. From the outside, it looks as if it were reasoning.

69
00:06:08,479 --> 00:06:10,361
Part two. Building it.

70
00:06:12,996 --> 00:06:15,048
How big is this machine?

71
00:06:15,298 --> 00:06:20,512
Each cog is an adjustable weight: a setting for how strongly it acts.

72
00:06:21,498 --> 00:06:25,231
A dozen cogs: not much of a language model.

73
00:06:25,898 --> 00:06:29,801
GPT-2, in 2019, had a hundred and twenty-four million weights.

74
00:06:31,498 --> 00:06:35,031
Each attention head looks back in its own way.

75
00:06:36,098 --> 00:06:40,942
Then sizes exploded. In 2020, GPT-3 reached a hundred and seventy-five billion.

76
00:06:44,798 --> 00:06:48,331
Today's largest models go beyond, to hundreds of billions.

77
00:06:48,581 --> 00:06:56,418
Why so many? Because language is immense: grammar, knowledge, situations, styles. A model of language has billions of knobs.

78
00:06:57,879 --> 00:07:04,776
Who sets billions of knobs? In traditional software, people do: a programmer specifies each step by hand.

79
00:07:07,679 --> 00:07:13,464
Machine learning goes the other way around. Nobody sets the knobs. The data does.

80
00:07:13,714 --> 00:07:21,351
Take one example. The machine predicts "house", but the true next token is "coat". That error can be measured.

81
00:07:21,601 --> 00:07:30,150
It travels back through the machine, and each cog is adjusted a little, to reduce it, layer by layer. This is backpropagation.

82
00:07:30,400 --> 00:07:35,814
Run the example again, and the error shrinks. A few rounds later: "coat".

83
00:07:37,829 --> 00:07:43,414
Now do that with almost everything ever written: the web, books, blogs, news, code.

84
00:07:43,664 --> 00:07:46,827
Recipes, court rulings, poems, football reports, software manuals.

85
00:07:53,720 --> 00:08:04,150
Each text becomes a series of guessing games: hide the next token, then guess it. Nobody has to label anything: the text holds its own answers.

86
00:08:04,400 --> 00:08:09,415
That makes trillions of problems, solved in parallel on huge clusters of computers.

87
00:08:09,665 --> 00:08:15,050
At first, the guesses are poor, and every mistake sends corrections to the cogs.

88
00:08:15,300 --> 00:08:16,241
Fewer mistakes.

89
00:08:19,822 --> 00:08:20,762
Fewer still.

90
00:08:21,171 --> 00:08:24,163
This is pre-training: the P in GPT.

91
00:08:24,971 --> 00:08:34,460
What gets tuned? The transformer layers, the T in GPT, and the embedding table. As models grow, the layers take almost all the weights.

92
00:08:36,162 --> 00:08:39,155
Now, a confession: there are no cogs.

93
00:08:39,405 --> 00:08:43,138
They were only a picture. These are electronic machines.

94
00:08:44,505 --> 00:08:49,349
A stack of dials is really a list of numbers: a vector.

95
00:08:49,599 --> 00:08:54,444
A column of cogs is really a grid of numbers: the weights.

96
00:08:54,694 --> 00:09:00,820
And all of this runs on chips called GPUs, built for exactly this kind of arithmetic.

97
00:09:01,070 --> 00:09:07,936
A layer's weights are loaded from memory, thousands of cores multiply and add, and the numbers move on.

98
00:09:08,186 --> 00:09:20,268
One token, through a model like GPT-3, takes about a hundred and seventy-five billion operations. By hand, five and a half thousand years. On a GPU, a fraction of a millisecond.

99
00:09:20,518 --> 00:09:25,532
Training repeats this a hundred thousand times, on thousands of chips, for weeks.

100
00:09:25,782 --> 00:09:32,108
Scale is only possible at the speed of silicon. Not cogs anymore, but still a machine.

101
00:09:33,962 --> 00:09:36,585
Part three. From machine to assistant.

102
00:09:39,679 --> 00:09:46,375
After pre-training, the model is a superb text completer, but not an assistant. Ask it a question…

103
00:09:46,625 --> 00:09:53,322
…and it just carries on, the way a poem might. A likely continuation, not what you wanted.

104
00:09:53,572 --> 00:09:55,994
This is a raw, non-aligned model.

105
00:09:58,572 --> 00:10:02,105
To make it helpful, we open the machine again.

106
00:10:04,402 --> 00:10:10,528
We train it on examples of good answers, including when the right answer is to decline.

107
00:10:16,708 --> 00:10:20,812
Same machine, same cogs. Far fewer examples, much smaller nudges.

108
00:10:21,271 --> 00:10:23,323
Then, better answers get rewarded.

109
00:10:23,573 --> 00:10:26,566
This is alignment. Same machine, new behaviour.

110
00:10:29,803 --> 00:10:30,744
Same question…

111
00:10:34,786 --> 00:10:36,838
…and this time, an answer.

112
00:10:38,636 --> 00:10:44,592
It's still a next-token guesser. But now, the likely next tokens are a helpful answer.

113
00:10:46,145 --> 00:10:50,990
So why do these models seem so good? Here's a proposed interpretation.

114
00:10:51,240 --> 00:10:55,714
Take a request: a web app for a bike repair shop.

115
00:10:55,964 --> 00:11:03,401
In seconds, it writes a whole project, clean and structured. A person would still be on the first lines.

116
00:11:03,651 --> 00:11:07,384
And it's not generic: it's adapted to the need.

117
00:11:08,351 --> 00:11:12,995
A trained expert may do better in places, but rarely this fast.

118
00:11:13,245 --> 00:11:21,623
Why? First, because thousands of people have solved almost the same problems, and published their work. The model has integrated it.

119
00:11:21,873 --> 00:11:28,940
Second, because of scale: billions of knobs, tuned until it guesses well in an enormous number of contexts.

120
00:11:29,804 --> 00:11:38,182
But look closer. This library does not exist. Examples were few here, and a likely guess is not a guaranteed one.

121
00:11:38,432 --> 00:11:42,335
So you check, you report it, and it gets fixed.

122
00:11:42,882 --> 00:11:47,726
Often a very good first draft. An efficient jumpstarter, and knowledge gatherer.

123
00:11:49,879 --> 00:11:54,723
What if it could check its own work? Give it a task.

124
00:11:54,973 --> 00:12:04,462
The machine still only writes text. But now a program runs that text, and the result comes back into its context. That's an agent.

125
00:12:04,712 --> 00:12:16,253
It runs the tests, and one fails. It reads the error, corrects the code, tries again. It's now grounded in what actually happens. That raises confidence, without guaranteeing anything.

126
00:12:17,992 --> 00:12:21,896
Step by step, it can walk through much longer tasks.

127
00:12:25,900 --> 00:12:32,967
But each next token is still a likely guess. And sometimes, the wheel lands on a bad one.

128
00:12:33,217 --> 00:12:39,173
So we limit what it can reach: whatever tries to leave the fence gets stopped.

129
00:12:39,423 --> 00:12:42,586
And for what really matters, a human decides.

130
00:12:42,923 --> 00:12:47,767
"Almost right" is fine in a draft. It's risky in an action.

131
00:12:49,567 --> 00:12:52,560
So, let's ask again. Who are you?
